---
// THE FOUR WINDS STEWARDSHIP-CIRCLE DECK (founder 2026-09-01) — supersedes
// /weave/audacitylabs-4winds (which now 308s here). Audience: the coalition
// of nonprofits Andrea is bringing together to steward the Four Winds
// property. Purpose: show the other players what Lichen IS and the role it
// holds in the collaboration — an orientation, not a capital ask.
//
// ⚠ NO FINANCIAL CONTENT, deliberately: the $4.8M figure and the
// owner-financing section from the superseded deck belonged to a different
// room and are omitted here per the standing rule (no financial claims
// without the founder's explicit per-deck go-ahead).
//
// Copy provenance: the land/hub/technology/economy passages are the
// founder-approved copy from the superseded deck, reframed for the circle;
// the role/of-scope sections are new. The struck-seat pattern stays — this
// audience IS the partners who fill the open chairs.
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import WeavePitch from '../components/WeavePitch.astro';
import FourWindsPhones from '../components/FourWindsPhones.astro';
import TangoScene from '../components/TangoScene.astro';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Four Winds · Lichen"
  description="For the circle of organizations stewarding Four Winds: what Lichen is, and the role it holds in the collaboration."
>
  <WeavePitch partner="the Four Winds stewardship circle">
    <Fragment slot="ecosystem">
      <FourWindsPhones />
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="economy">
      <TangoScene kids />
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="weave">
      <span class="eyebrow">The Four Winds collaboration</span>

      <h3 class="display h-2 wsub">The land at the center</h3>

      <p class="wbody">
        Thirty-five acres against the Front Range, between Pikes Peak and
        Longs Peak. The 1899 Wolfensberger barn, whose acoustics are said to
        rival Carnegie Hall. Pastures, ponds, ancient pines and dark skies.
        Horses, llamas, goats and a wild mustang, alongside the deer, elk,
        fox and owls that were already here. A regenerative farm with
        agricultural status, an organic garden and greenhouse, lodging and
        practitioner housing, and fifteen years of programming for veterans,
        first responders, trauma survivors, people with special needs, and
        anyone else who came to grow. It is also KAHUBA&rsquo;s headquarters
        — seventeen years of sanctuary work, 45+ rescued horses, llamas,
        goats, dogs and feral cats given a forever home. This becomes the
        first property held in a purpose-formed land trust: the land
        can&rsquo;t be sold out from under the work, and the work has
        somewhere permanent to stand.
      </p>

      <p class="wbody">
        No single organization stewards a place like this. Andrea is
        gathering a circle — healing programming, education, animal
        sanctuary, land care, business operations — each sovereign, each
        bringing what it already does well. This page is Lichen&rsquo;s
        introduction to that circle: what we are, what we hold, and what we
        deliberately don&rsquo;t.
      </p>

      <h3 class="display h-2 wsub">The role Lichen holds</h3>

      <h4 class="display worg">The property&rsquo;s operating system</h4>
      <p class="wbody">
        Every room and thing Four Winds stewards goes onto a real calendar —
        the barn, the arena, the therapy rooms, the guest house, the stables,
        the truck, the long tables. What the sanctuary already has stops
        sitting idle, and anyone booking it does so through a steward who
        approves each request. The land itself is a member, not a backdrop: a
        beyond-human entity whose contributions are recorded, and whose care
        is funded. Scheduling, membership, care coordination, events and
        shared calendars run on one platform instead of a dozen
        spreadsheets.
      </p>

      <h4 class="display worg">The economy that counts what&rsquo;s already happening</h4>
      <p class="wbody">
        Walking through the operating history, the striking thing isn&rsquo;t
        the costs. It&rsquo;s how much of Four Winds already runs on exactly
        what Lichen was built to count. Land care happens through work-trade.
        A neighbour farms the fields in exchange for half the crop. The vet
        donates her services because she loves what the education centre
        does. Students get paid to stack hay. Llama fibre and pellets hold
        the agricultural status. Volunteers arrive, and some of them stay.
      </p>
      <p class="wbody">
        None of that appears in any ledger. It is a reciprocity economy
        already operating in the analog — held together by Andrea&rsquo;s
        relationships and fifteen years of goodwill, and invisible the moment
        she steps back. The gap isn&rsquo;t generosity; Four Winds has never
        lacked that. The gap is that nobody has ever <em>counted</em> it,
        which means it can&rsquo;t be seen by a funder, scaled to another
        property, or handed to a successor. That is precisely the gap
        Current-cy closes: every hour, every bale, every trade recorded and
        returned to the person who gave it.
      </p>

      <h4 class="display worg">The web every partner is woven into</h4>
      <p class="wbody">
        Each organization in the circle gets its own presence on the
        platform: a real website on the open web — programmes, offerings, a
        calendar, bookable sessions, a team — updating itself as the work
        changes, because it draws from the same listings members already see.
        Your people become members of one shared network; your events land on
        shared calendars; contributions across organizations are counted in
        one economy rather than scattered across goodwill and memory.
      </p>

      <h3 class="display h-2 wsub">What Lichen deliberately does not hold</h3>

      <p class="wbody">
        A circle works when every seat is honestly named — including the
        empty ones. Lichen&rsquo;s scope here is the technology, economy and
        network layer. These seats belong to partners:
      </p>

      <ol class="wpaths">
        <li>
          <s class="wstrike"><strong>Healing programming.</strong> The care
          delivered on the land — clinical, equine, somatic, ceremonial —
          belongs to the practitioners and organizations who bring it.</s>
          <span class="wslot">&#10216; a healing partner &#10217;</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <s class="wstrike"><strong>Education.</strong> Young people growing
          up alongside land, animals and a working farm, learning stewardship
          and reciprocity by living them.</s>
          <span class="wslot">&#10216; an education partner &#10217;</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <s class="wstrike"><strong>Business &amp; day-to-day operations.</strong>
          The land-trust structure that receives what&rsquo;s given and holds
          the ground, and the business layer that runs the property day to
          day.</s>
          <span class="wslot">&#10216; an operations partner &#10217;</span>
        </li>
      </ol>

      <p class="wbody">
        Sovereignty is the design, not a compromise: each organization keeps
        its own board, finances, donors and identity. The platform is where
        the collaboration becomes visible and countable — never who&rsquo;s
        in charge of it.
      </p>

      <h3 class="display h-2 wsub">What the pilot proves</h3>

      <p class="wbody">
        Four Winds is uniquely ready to be first — healed land, turnkey
        operations, legally and agriculturally established. It is the pilot:
        the place where Lichen&rsquo;s model stops being an idea and becomes
        an address people can visit, measure and participate in. Every
        organization in the circle is a partner in that pilot, not a tenant
        of it. What each partner learns here travels to every property that
        follows.
      </p>

      <p class="wcallout">
        The land can&rsquo;t be sold out from under the work. That is the
        whole point, and it is the one promise no ordinary sale can make.
      </p>

      <h3 class="display h-2 wsub">Where this starts</h3>

      <p class="wbody">
        Concretely, and soon: your organization gets its page and calendar on
        the platform, your people join as alpha members, and the
        contributions already flowing at Four Winds start being counted. The
        circle fills its open seats in its own time — the table is set either
        way.
      </p>
    </Fragment>
  </WeavePitch>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .worg {
    font-size: var(--t-xl); font-weight: 400; color: var(--ink);
    margin-top: var(--s-6);
  }
  .wstrike { text-decoration: line-through; text-decoration-thickness: 1.5px; color: var(--ink-faint); }
  .wstrike strong { color: var(--ink-faint); }
  .wslot {
    display: inline-block; margin-left: 6px;
    padding: 2px 12px; border: 1.5px dashed var(--peach);
    border-radius: var(--r-full);
    font-style: italic; font-size: var(--t-sm); color: var(--peach-deep);
    white-space: nowrap;
  }
</style>
